{%- comment -%}Load this section's styles only when the section renders.{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'th-section-product-reviews.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{% comment %}
  Product reviews (Figma: Reviews, 508:10348).

  The heading, and under it the shop's Judge.me review widget wearing the kit's
  clothes. The section owns the heading and the arrangement; everything with a
  review in it is Judge.me's app block, dropped into `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`
  by the merchant.

  ## Why the widget is a block and not markup here

  Judge.me renders the score, the rating breakdown, the review list, the sort
  control, the filter panel and the write-a-review form. None of it can be rebuilt
  in Liquid — the reviews are not in the theme — so the honest arrangement is the
  app block inside our layout, restyled. `"blocks": [{ "type": "@app" }]` is what
  lets it land here; the merchant adds "Review widget" inside this section rather
  than beside it.

  ## Half of this design is a setting, not a stylesheet

  Two choices in Judge.me's own dashboard do more for the file than any amount of
  CSS could, and both are set on this shop:

  - **Widget header → Theme: Standard.** This is what draws the 5→1 rating
    breakdown. The Minimal header has no breakdown at all — no element, and no
    per-rating count anywhere on the page or in the `judgeme.*` metafields — so on
    Minimal the file's bar chart is not styling that is missing, it is data that
    does not exist.
  - **Reviews section → Theme: Align.** This is what puts the reviewer in a column
    to the left of the review text, which is how the file draws a row. Before it,
    that column was a grid built with `display: contents` over three of Judge.me's
    wrappers, and it was the most fragile thing in this file.

  So if a row ever loses its left column, or the breakdown disappears, look at
  those two settings before looking here.

  ## The trap: Judge.me writes its palette inline

  Every `--jm-*` value is an inline style on `.jm-review-widget`, so a stylesheet
  rule for one loses no matter how specific it is. The colours are all set in the
  dashboard now and need no rule; what is left is the corner radius and the bar's
  thickness, whose settings the dashboard keeps behind its paid tier, and the width
  the app block writes onto itself. Those four are the only `!important` in this
  file, and inline styles are why.

  ## The other trap: their rules are a class plus a scope attribute

  Judge.me's own selectors are `.jm-thing[data-v-…]`, which is (0,2,0), and their
  stylesheet is a `<link>` the app injects after the theme's bundle. A rule of ours
  at (0,2,0) ties and then loses on order, so every rule below carries
  `.jm-review-widget` for weight rather than for scope.

  ## What is not styled here, and why

  The file draws a star row under the score. Judge.me's summary prints the number
  and the review count and has no stars element, and the score is not exposed on
  any attribute a rule could read, so there is nothing to style and nothing to
  build it from. The number is stated, and the breakdown beside it carries a star
  on every row.

  The file also draws "Was this helpful?" with a thumb either side. That is
  Judge.me's "Show review thumbs up and down", which their dashboard keeps behind
  the paid tier — a switch, not a stylesheet, so it is not worked around here.
{% endcomment %}

<div
  class="section section--page-width spacing-style color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}"
  style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
  data-testid="product-reviews-{{ section.id }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div
    class="product-reviews"
    style="
      --product-reviews-gap: {{ section.settings.gap_mobile }}px;
      --product-reviews-gap-desktop: {{ section.settings.gap }}px;
      {%- comment -%}
        The check glyph for the Verified tag. It is handed over as a custom
        property because a `{% stylesheet %}` block is static CSS — Liquid is not
        run inside one, so `asset_url` cannot be written where the rule needs it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      --product-reviews-verified-icon: url('{{ 'icon-ui-badge-check.svg' | asset_url }}');
    "
  >
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_ui-heading', id: 'heading' %}

    <div class="product-reviews__widget">
      {% content_for 'blocks' %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>



{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product reviews",
  "tag": "section",
  "blocks": [{ "type": "@app" }],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.layout"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap",
      "label": "t:settings.gap",
      "min": 16,
      "max": 96,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 48
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_mobile",
      "label": "t:content.mobile_size",
      "min": 16,
      "max": 96,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.colors"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "t:settings.color_scheme",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "t:settings.top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 200,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 80
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "t:settings.bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 200,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 80
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Product reviews",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "_ui-heading",
          "static": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<h2>What <em>customers say</em></h2>",
            "max_width": 510
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
